# M5 fold-recall A/B (release, ec_distann_m2, real 10k corpus subset)
# head SHA: 6fcecd384, 2001-row working set, graph_degree=32, 50 queries
# A_full = full build on all 2001 rows; B_fold = build on 1801 + insert 200 + fold
# both indexes cover the identical 2001 rows (verified b_rows=a_rows=2001)
# cmd: ecaz dev sql --db ec_distann_m2 --file fold-recall.sql

index  | recall@10
A_full | 0.9560
B_fold | 0.8900

# FINDING: the fold is functionally correct (folded rows found via graph
# traversal, per the pg_test) but costs ~0.066 recall@10 vs a full rebuild
# at this config (200/2001 = ~21% of the index folded). Attributable to the
# append-if-free backlink limitation (full neighbors skip the back-edge) +
# head-sample-only candidate search (folded nodes don't seed each other).
# The full-reprune-backlink + head-sample-refresh follow-ups target this gap.

# --- Rejected hypothesis (measured, not assumed) ---
# Tried: interconnect a fold batch (already-folded nodes seed later folds so
# they connect to each other, not just the built graph). Prediction: closes
# the gap. RESULT: recall got WORSE, 0.8900 -> 0.8420. Interconnecting folds
# builds a poorly-anchored island of folded nodes (their forward edges go to
# folded siblings instead of anchoring to the well-connected built graph).
# Change reverted. The real lever is backlink QUALITY into the built graph
# (full-reprune when a neighbor is at capacity), not candidate interconnection.

# --- Full-reprune backlinks (measured, KEPT) ---
# Fix: at capacity, reprune the neighbor's adjacency (plan_insert_backlink) so a
# folded node earns an INCOMING edge from the built graph (evicting the
# neighbor's worst edge) instead of being skipped. Distinct from the rejected
# interconnection: this adds built->folded edges (reachability), not
# folded<->folded edges.
# RESULT: B_fold recall@10 0.8900 -> 0.8980 (gap 0.066 -> 0.058). A real +0.008
# improvement; correctness holds (101 distann pg_tests pass). KEPT.
# Residual gap is the head-sample-only candidate search (a proper greedy insert
# search past the head-index cap is the scaling follow-up); REINDEX still
# restores full parity.
